the rsd we've been created more like  what i would call like Swift boats where  we're able to change directions nimbly  and quickly in traditional school  systems have been like luxury liners if  you want to turn that thing around it  takes forever and a day I'm Catherine  manga award with reason TV and we're  here at the national summit on education  reform with patrick joe' bard who is the  superintendent of the louisiana recovery  school district thank you for joining us  thank you for having me you were  horribly unlucky and also very lucky to  have a weather disaster which kind of  kicked off what happened in you in  norleans and in Louisiana more generally  I'm for people who don't know the story  tell me the story of how the recovery  school district exists the recovery  school district exists in 2003 our  legislature actually passed the law that  said the state would be able to  intervene in chronically underperforming  schools and then in 2005 you know  catastrophe occurred a disaster just  beyond our wildest imagination and  basically wiped the city of New Orleans  out just for a period of time and so in  2005 after the storm we had a special  legislative session where the  legislature at that time felt that it  was important to use this as an  opportunity to really try to transform  the education landscape in the water  before the stone we saw a public school  system that was really in total  dysfunction and disarray so much so that  you know the valedictorian of one of the  high schools wasn't able to pass a state  minimum skills assessment test in order  to earn her diploma and so Katrina  literally and figuratively washed away  many of the institutional barriers that  had prevented us from even imagining  that we could begin to make some  systemic changes to this school system  so I think one element of what you do  and what has helped kind of improve  schools really anywhere in the country  is the closing of bad schools right  that's something that it's never popular  even when a school is bad you know still  parents are you do it's my school  sometimes people have kind of a feeling  of ownership and of course teachers  unions tend to not like it can you tell  me about some schools that you  closed and why sure one in particular  was a school last year at this time that  we made a decision to close it was  Benjamin Mays prep school so charter  school that had been in existence for  five years they were coming up on their  renewal for their contract and it didn't  meet any of the bars as far as like the  criteria so you have to have like  different areas of academic achievement  financial solvency and for us it was a  matter of saying you know we went to  parents and talk to them and parents  were upset about the school closing but  it was a classic example of what some  have coined the happy bad school so our  parents might have been engaged and the  kids engaged and they felt good kids  weren't reading on level they weren't  moving as their cohorts around the city  were moving in other schools and so it  was us to be able to say look your kids  deserve better we can still make it a  happy school but it can be a good school  and with that we close the school there  was some you know complaints and people  upset but those kids who then took us up  on the offer and those families to get  them into high-performing schools over  eighty five percent of the parents that  took advantage of it got into a higher  performing school that very next year  and so it works so we have to try to  balance and be sensitive to community  concerns but at the end of the day we  value having students in higher quality  environments we're going to do  everything we can within the law to  ensure that that happens do you see  other places where there's kind of a  concern that as you know as the the  Charter systems kind of regular eyes and  as people just get used to the status  quo that there might just be kind of a  creepy creeping ree centralization I  mean that's sort of a natural tendency  of bureaucracies what do you do to  prevent that yeah I think I think a  couple of things will happen to prevent  that one our charters will remind us of  their autonomy and in really you know  and say hey this might be an  infringement I think it would keep us  like on our toes to not become the  bureaucracies that some of us have come  to [ __ ] but I think it's one of those  things where the rsd we've been created  more like what i will call like Swift  boats where we're able to change  directions nimbly and quickly and  traditional school systems have been  like luxury liners if you want to turn  that thing around it takes forever and a  day and so  for us whether it's an infringement on  autonomy at least we're having  conversations that leads us down the  path to like be cognizant of it to  define it and then figure out if it's  best for the collective of schools  versus just you know turning into that  luxury line again so i think we'll  continue to be like Swift boats and be  able to make these nimble decisions but  we'll be able to do it as a whole and we  will police ourselves on that what is  one thing that you personally do on a  regular basis in your job that other  superintendents don't do I meet with a  lot of different stakeholders I probably  meet with critics as much as I do  support us because I really want to get  that information and that feedback to  really help us leverage all of our  resources in order to better serve kids  and so I think more than anything the  majority of my time is meeting with  stakeholders meeting with school leaders  meeting with community group organizers  meeting with students at times to make  certain that I stay grounded in the  everyday operations of schools so  therefore when our team is working  together we can make in foreign policies  and I think another thing that I do I  encourage my teams to stay in schools  like if we don't constantly go to  schools and really be at Ground Zero to  see what's happening every day you know  we can't make good decisions as  policymakers because we're too far  removed and we're only acting on what we  think we know versus what we know we  know are there any schools left toward  the valedictorian can't pass state  standards not at this time there thanks  very much for speaking with us thank you  for having me appreciate it for reason  TV I'm Katherine Maggie ward with  Patrick debarred of the Louisiana  recovery school district  you
 

